ROW 15 KMS and local GPG "independent failure domains … fail for uncorrelated reasons … durability does not rest on any single domain surviving"
docs/phase-0-prereg.md:292: C signs the same canonical bytes with two keys from *independent failure
docs/phase-0-prereg.md:293: domains* — KMS (cloud-native custody) and local GPG (Willay's portable WHO
docs/phase-0-prereg.md:294: layer). They fail for *uncorrelated* reasons (AWS account/policy lapse vs. local
docs/phase-0-prereg.md:295: key disclosure), so the attestation's durability does not rest on any single
docs/phase-0-prereg.md:296: domain surviving. **Not replicating is what concentrates the risk**; the cost of
docs/phase-0-prereg.md:297: C over B is essentially nil, and the benefit is that attestations stand up to
docs/phase-0-prereg.md:298: adjudication even if one trust domain disappears entirely.
