   111	**Normative expansion (the mapping clause).** "Continuous accepted
   112	evidentiary chain" means **exactly** the following witness chain, and
   113	nothing weaker:
   114	
   115	> accepted external authority evidence
   116	> → the exact authority statement consumed from the manifest
   117	> → issuer identity and signing key
   118	> → proof of possession **by that same key**
   119	> → accepted signature verifying under that key
   120	> → the exact framed bytes
   121	
   122	Both forms are registered — the sentence is the property statement the
   123	relying-party story and the written proof carry; the chain is the
   124	proof obligation the capstone (§A3.3) discharges. The mapping clause
   125	binds them: neither may drift from the other. (Author ruling
   126	2026-08-07: the two formulations are complementary altitudes, not
   127	rivals; the rationale — report what was done, defer interpretation to
   128	future verifiers and their risk tolerance — is on the record in the
   129	2026-07-28 ruling doc.)
   130	
   131	**Why linked, not counted (registered rationale).** Counted floors
   132	("≥1 accepted signature AND ≥1 accepted external evidence") admit the
   133	two-worlds attack: evidence supports manifest M′ while the accepted
   134	signature claims authority from M; both counters pass with the chain
   135	broken — the aTLS genus. And without any floor, the model's
   136	IssuanceEvent correspondence becomes privileged historical knowledge
   137	unavailable to a real verifier: the proof would be of the wrong model.
   138	
   139	**Interaction with the A1.2.1 waiver lattice (tightening, stated
   140	exactly):**
   141	
   142	1. A1.2.1's waivable item "accepting fewer than all external
   143	   manifest-authority evidences (P10)" is tightened to: **fewer, but
   144	   never zero** — redundant members of the A1.5 evidence set may be
   145	   waived; the final complete chain may not be broken or eliminated.
   146	2. **Unavailability is not waiver.** If every external authority
   147	   evidence for a layer is unavailable, unsupported, or otherwise
   148	   unperformable, the layer's verdict is `UNVERIFIABLE` — never
   149	   `VALID_DEGRADED`. Evidence that is present and whose validation is
   150	   performed but fails yields `INVALID`. The P4 partition applies
   151	   inside the floor exactly as A1.2.1 already requires of
   152	   non-waivable checks.
   153	3. **Proof of possession is chain-internal:** the possession proof
   154	   must be by the same key whose signature is accepted (consistent
   155	   with A1.5's rule that the self-signature is never an authority
   156	   channel). An existential self-signature by another key in a
   157	   multi-key manifest does not close the chain.
   158	4. **Per-layer quantification.** A wrapped package's inner and outer
   159	   attestations are separately typed signed objects (P7); each layer
   160	   contributing to a returned valid verdict carries its own complete
   161	   chain, evaluated independently. The base assessment reports each
   162	   layer's result without replacement by an aggregate judgment; a
   163	   higher-level relying-party policy may consume those results. The
   164	   proof obligation quantifies over every contributing layer.
   165	5. **No change to P4's partition.** The floors bound entry into the
   166	   two VALID states; they add no states and alter no verdict
   167	   semantics. P4 remains correct but incomplete by abstraction.
   168	
   169	### A3.2.1 The chain's first-link invariant
   170	
   171	A1 §A1.5's two external authority evidences (the DNSSEC chain snapshot
   172	and the anchored repository publication, both archived in the bundle)
   173	publish **issuer-key fingerprints**, not manifest digests. Registering
   174	an exact-manifest-digest mechanism here would both narrow the design
   175	space prematurely and risk a self-reference if the hashed manifest
   176	itself carries the archived evidence.
   177	
   178	The requirement is therefore stated at the security boundary:
   179	
   180	> External authority evidence must unambiguously bind the exact
   181	> authority statement the verifier consumes. Two manifests that differ
   182	> in any authority-relevant fact must not be supportable by the same
   183	> evidence; differences explicitly declared non-authoritative may remain
   184	> outside that binding.
   185	
   186	The mechanism spike may realize this with a separately hashed manifest
   187	core, an exact authorized tuple, or another construction. It must name
   188	which manifest facts are authority-relevant and carry a broken companion
   189	in which two authority-distinct manifests share evidence; the consuming
   190	linkage query must fail. The mechanism is not registered until the spike
   191	has supplied that evidence.
   192	
   193	`[AUTHOR DECISION — ratified - 2026-08-08]` **Override of adopted text,
   194	named.** The adopted record (the 07-28 ruling, Ruling 2 item 5)
   195	docketed exactly two options — digest publication or authorized
   196	tuple — required A3 to choose between them, and stated that the exact
   197	links are resolved in A3 before the spike. This section declines both
   198	options in favor of the boundary invariant above and moves mechanism
   199	selection after the spike. Rationale: each docketed option is a
   200	premature narrowing, and the digest option additionally risks
   201	self-reference (verified against A1 §A1.5: the manifest carries the
   202	archived authority evidences). The invariant with its two-worlds
   203	broken companion preserves the falsifiable content the docketed fork
   204	existed to protect. The same ruling covers the chain's second link:
   205	this amendment's "exact authority statement consumed from the
   206	manifest" supersedes the ruling doc's "exact manifest" link, and the
   207	spike targets the chain as registered here.
   208	
