   258	
   259	## A3.4 Survivability: three claims, a Designated Community, and the bundle
   260	
   261	**The claim "attestation survives Tessera's termination" is split into
   262	three registered claims with different truth conditions (adopted
   263	2026-08-07):**
   264	
   265	1. **Portability / self-containment:** a possessed bundle can be
   266	   verified without Tessera. Fully a Band 0/H1a obligation: the bundle
   267	   carries data (P7/P9/P10, A1.5, A2.2), an unambiguous identifier and
   268	   hash for the governing verification specification, and — added by
   269	   this amendment — the Tessera-specific procedure itself.
   270	2. **Availability:** a copy remains obtainable only through retention
   271	   and custody. Custody is necessary **immediately**, not merely past
   272	   the first algorithm death.
   273	3. **Long-horizon evidentiary continuity:** validation across
   274	   cryptographic decay requires renewal by custodians **before** the
   275	   old mechanism becomes unreliable — RFC 4998 (ERS) renewal, under an
   276	   RFC 4810-style cryptographic-maintenance policy; renewal wraps, it
   277	   never replaces (consistent with the wrap-don't-replace re-anchoring
   278	   already registered in the original §4).
   279	
   280	**The survivability floor (general, every artifact class).** For every
   281	artifact class Tessera produces, its survivability guarantees are
   282	limited to: creation and the protocol obligations it can observe;
   283	verifiable handoff when acknowledgment occurs; portable identity; and
   284	visible failure while an observer possessing the relevant state
   285	survives. Continued availability after handoff is conditional on
   286	custody. No construction can guarantee recovery after every holder of
   287	the complete artifact loses it. (The three claims above are this
   288	floor applied to receipts; §A3.7.2 applies it to refusal records.)
   289	
   290	**Bundle additions registered for claim 1** (from the 2026-07-27
   291	gap analysis and the 2026-08-08 author dialogue):
   292	
   293	- An unambiguous verification-spec identifier **and its hash bound into
   294	  the signed bytes** — a verifier can prove which procedure governs.
   295	- **The Tessera verification specification and its conformance vectors
   296	  embedded in each individual receipt bundle as typed, content-addressed
   297	  objects** (P7's enumerated type set gains the specification member;
   298	  the set already includes conformance vectors). A later aggregate
   299	  preservation package may deduplicate those immutable objects without
   300	  changing their identity or semantics.
   301	- **A plain-language purpose preamble** (WIPP-marker style):
   302	  typed and bound Representation Information for *intent*, so a future
   303	  reader knows what this object is for before knowing how to check it.
   304	
   305	**The Designated Community declaration:**
   306	
   307	> A member of the Designated Community — human, machine, or
   308	> collaboration — can reconstruct Tessera verification and interpret
   309	> its bounded result using only the preserved receipt bundle and the
   310	> exact external standards dependencies declared by that bundle,
   311	> without Tessera-specific institutional knowledge. This
   312	> reconstructability claim is scoped to survivability claim 1 alone;
   313	> it promises nothing about availability or evidentiary continuity.
   314	> For horizons
   315	> requiring claims 2 and 3, the Designated Community is not only the
   316	> bundle's *audience* but its *custodian*: availability and evidentiary
   317	> continuity are declared custodial dependencies, named here rather
   318	> than inherited silently from the ERS citation. Future decision makers
   319	> may amend the custodial practice as they learn; the dependency itself
   320	> is permanent and honest.
   321	
   322	**The century horizon is aspirational, not a guarantee or membership
   323	criterion.** Tessera is designed to preserve reconstructability across
   324	successive technology changes for as long as custodians continue the
   325	declared work. It makes no prediction that today's bundle, standards,
   326	algorithms, or competence declaration will suffice one century hence.
   327	
   328	`[AUTHOR DECISION — revised - 2026-08-08]` **Competence profile (the
   329	rotation baseline).** A Designated Community member can, using
   330	ordinary documented computing tools: interpret the bundle's declared
   331	public-standard dependencies; implement or execute the deterministic
   332	Tessera verification procedure; run and interpret its positive,
   333	negative, and broken conformance cases; and distinguish each
   334	structured assessment dimension from payload meaning. No
   335	Tessera-specific institutional knowledge is assumed. Every custodial
   336	challenge declares any prerequisite beyond this baseline; failure on
   337	a challenge exceeding it does not by itself establish a bundle
   338	defect. This is the baseline §A3.5's challenge-rotation reading (b)
   339	measures against — and what an amendment revises when that reading
   340	is ruled.
   341	
   342	**Dependency closure is layered:**
   343	
   344	1. **Tessera verification dependencies** — framing,
   345	   canonicalization, signatures, hashes, temporal proofs, and evidence
   346	   formats needed to verify Tessera's own claim. Tessera owns this
   347	   closure. The bundle identifies each exact external standard edition,
   348	   the normative sections used, known-errata disposition, stable digest
   349	   where canonical bytes exist, retrieval identifier, redistribution
   350	   status, and failure consequence. Tessera-authored procedure and
   351	   conformance artifacts are embedded; external standards are embedded
   352	   opportunistically when lawful, otherwise precisely referenced.
   353	2. **Issuance-profile dependencies** — additional mechanisms chosen by
   354	   an issuer or deployment profile. The profile author declares them;
   355	   Tessera requires an unambiguous cryptographic binding and exposes
   356	   unavailable interpretation as `UNVERIFIABLE` for the affected check.
   357	3. **Payload-interpretation dependencies** — schemas, vocabularies,
   358	   software, ontologies, codecs, or contextual material supplied by the
   359	   submitter as an optional typed Representation Information companion.
   360	   The receipt commits to the companion's exact bytes. Tessera preserves
   361	   its identity but does not assert its correctness, sufficiency,
   362	   legality, availability, or the payload meaning it proposes. Absence
   363	   or uninterpretability affects payload interpretability, never the P4
   364	   envelope verdict.
   365	
   366	Continued availability and correct interpretation of precisely
   367	referenced but unembedded standards are exposed Designated Community
   368	assumptions. An unavailable normative dependency is a visible
   369	preservation failure. Our goal is to always disclose such dependencies.
   370	
   371	**Demonstrations registered:** the dead-service test (P9: verify with
   372	Tessera unreachable) and the dead-project test (clean-room: a verifier
   373	with no project-specific context receives bundle plus broken
   374	companions only, must reconstruct the procedure and reach correct
   375	verdicts including the INVALID/UNVERIFIABLE traps). Each dead-project
   376	run records its **evaluation conditions** — verifier provenance,
   377	challenge creation time, disclosure state, candidate access — because
   378	recency reduces prior-exposure risk but proves nothing (§A3.5).
   379	
   380	---
   381	
   382	## A3.5 Tending: custodial attestations and challenge rotation
   383	
   384	**The tending obligation.** Tending records custodial assessment, not
   385	an assumption that every assessment produces a renewal artifact. The
   386	record has one of four outcomes:
   387	
   388	1. `NO_ACTION_REQUIRED` — the custodian evaluated the declared
   389	   triggers and recorded that none had fired;
   390	2. `RENEWED` — a trigger fired, or discretionary renewal occurred; the
   391	   record references the resulting renewal artifact and its predecessor
   392	   linkage is mechanically checked;
   393	3. `ACTION_DUE` — renewal is required but has not yet completed; or
   394	4. `FAILED` — an attempted required renewal did not complete.
   395	
   396	The last two outcomes make the gap visible rather than issuing a
   397	reassuring heartbeat.
   398	
   399	Every outcome exposes the policy and version applied; observations and
   400	their provenance; mechanically evaluated trigger results;
   401	judgment-based conclusions; unavailable observations; and, when
   402	applicable, the renewal-artifact reference and predecessor-link result.
   403	The record proves only that a named custodian made the recorded
   404	assessment. Mechanical checks prove only their bounded results; they do
   405	not make a judgment-based `NO_ACTION_REQUIRED` conclusion true.
   406	
   407	**Conditional visibility, not predicted safety.** Custodial policy
   408	declares renewal triggers and intended safety margins. Given an
   409	available monitoring clock and custodial record, crossing a trigger
   410	without the required tending record produces a visible stale condition.
   411	The claim that a chosen trigger precedes an unforeseen failure of the
   412	protected mechanism is an exposed operational assumption, periodically
   413	reassessed — never a Tessera guarantee.
   414	
   415	Custodial-health monitoring and any relying party holding a current
   416	custodial record may observe the condition. The stateless verifier of an
   417	old bundle cannot: silence is not contained in the bundle. Tending
   418	health therefore never changes a receipt's P4 verdict or protocol
   419	standing. Exact trigger expressions, cadence, clock, monitoring surface,
   420	and succession mechanism remain design obligations. They receive their
   421	own trust assumptions and do not reuse δ, ε, or k merely because the
   422	temporal modeling pattern is familiar.
   423	
   424	`[AUTHOR DECISION — ratified -- 2026-08-08]` **Status against the adopted
   425	four questions.** The adopted ruling (§4 of the 2026-08-07 record)
   426	made answering four questions the registrability condition for this
   427	obligation. As registered here: who observes — the custodial monitor
   428	and any holder of a current custodial record (above); what staleness
   429	affects — custodial-health reporting only, never a receipt verdict
   430	(above); where the cadence is declared — in the custodial policy.
   431	The clock question is answered as **clock roles, not one clock**,
   432	consistent with A2.1's discipline (an earlier recommendation of raw
   433	chain time repeated the fused-clock error and was withdrawn in
   434	review: block timestamps are non-monotonic, chain time is existed-by
   435	evidence rather than elapsed time, and A2 assigns operational
   436	lifecycle decisions to the wall clock):
   437	
   438	> The custodial policy declares its trigger classes, observation
   439	> sources, uncertainty treatment, and any maximum assessment
   440	> interval. Operational observation time governs when an assessment
   441	> becomes due; external chain time supplies existed-by evidence for
   442	> tending and renewal artifacts. Staleness is evaluated relative to
   443	> the policy's declared operational clock and trust assumptions,
   444	> never inferred from raw block timestamps alone. No claim is made
   445	> that a chosen interval precedes unforeseen mechanism failure.
   446	
   447	The cadence parameter lives in each custodial policy or profile, not
   448	as one universal constant; what Band 0 ratifies is the required
   449	parameter schema and the clock-role invariants above. This satisfies
   450	the adopted condition — all four questions answered — while freezing
   451	no global duration before custodial practice exists.
   452	
   453	**Challenge rotation (adopted, 2026-08-07).** Conformance vectors are
   454	two layers with different survival properties: a **fixed floor** in
   455	the bundle (memorizable, and acceptably so — a floor need only catch
   456	a naive fail-open verifier), and **novelty minted outside the bundle
   457	as custodial practice**. A bundled generator cannot by itself
   458	guarantee non-exposure or replace custodial rotation; a generator
   459	with a large case space or undisclosed seed may remain useful as a
   460	testing mechanism. Custodial minting reduces prior-exposure and
   461	rote-memorization risk and records its evaluation conditions. When
   462	an existing verifier fails a new challenge, the failure is a
   463	**measurement with three readings**, disposed under amendment
   464	discipline: contamination exposed (the test working); the challenge
   465	exceeds the Designated Community's declared competence (discard it,
   466	or amend the declaration); the bundle genuinely lacks what the
   467	challenge needs (a real gap — fix the bundle).
   468	
