   522	## A3.7 Amendment 2 residues, closed or disposed
   523	
   524	1. **Standing/equivocation (the A2.0 residue).** The abandoned-anchor
   525	   artifact can leave two cryptographically valid issuance artifacts for
   526	   the same content with different declared times; A2.2 and A2.4 confine
   527	   standing without deduplicating content. Comparative visibility is
   528	   insufficient: a presenter of the abandoned artifact could omit a
   529	   later lineage record. The registered invariant is therefore:
   530	
   531	   > Cryptographic validity alone confers no protocol standing. Any
   532	   > artifact claiming standing must present verifiable standing
   533	   > evidence binding its issuance identity, attempt lineage, and
   534	   > terminal disposition. Missing standing evidence leaves the artifact
   535	   > evidentially admissible but without protocol standing.
   536	
   537	   A terminal lineage record, capability, transparency witness, or
   538	   another construction may discharge the invariant. The chosen
   539	   mechanism must be testable on an abandoned artifact presented alone,
   540	   not only when competing artifacts happen to meet.
   541	
   542	   Standing is an **orthogonal assessment dimension**, not a fifth P4
   543	   state. The base result reports at least `ESTABLISHED`, `ABSENT`, or
   544	   `UNVERIFIABLE`, with evidence and reasons, alongside the unchanged P4
   545	   envelope verdict. Thus an artifact may honestly be
   546	   `verification = VALID_STRICT` and `protocol_standing = ABSENT`.
   547	   Relying-party policy decides whether those facts meet its own
   548	   requirements; Tessera does not collapse them into one checkmark.
   549	
   550	   `[PANEL-DRIVEN CLARIFICATION — 2026-08-08, from the Kimi cold
   551	   read]` Standing is not a member of the A1.2.1 waiver lattice: it
   552	   is reported, never waived. No verifier policy, strict or degraded,
   553	   rewrites the reported standing value. A relying-party policy may
   554	   accept an artifact whose standing is `ABSENT` or `UNVERIFIABLE`,
   555	   but that acceptance is the policy's own recorded decision outside
   556	   the verdict — the same separation the lattice already enforces
   557	   between waiving a check and erasing its record.
   558	2. **Refusal records (the A2.3 split).** The abstract refusal state
   559	   was discharged atomic-and-latching in Band 0. A2.3 registered
   560	   durability, retrievability, and reporting of the refusal record and
   561	   deliberately deferred their assignment; this amendment assigns
   562	   them. The governing observation (2026-08-08 dialogue): a mortal
   563	   authority cannot guarantee that evidence of its own decision
   564	   becomes externally durable atomically with its local transition —
   565	   local refusal, record delivery, and public publication occur in
   566	   different failure domains, and the honest construction exposes that
   567	   partial order rather than claiming to close it.
   568	
   569	   **Refusal state.** `REFUSED` latches atomically when A2.3's
   570	   terminal condition occurs; it is final and independent of all later
   571	   reporting states. Delivery or publication failure cannot reopen
   572	   issuance, consume another attempt, or erase the refusal. The same
   573	   transition creates, as local durable state (creation is neither
   574	   delivery nor publication):
   575	
   576	   - the complete portable refusal record — attempt identity,
   577	     disposition, reasons that may be disclosed, and the evidence
   578	     needed to verify it;
   579	   - its non-identifying public commitment value;
   580	   - delivery status `PENDING`; and
   581	   - publication status `PENDING`.
   582	
   583	   **Record handoff.** The generating authority retains the complete
   584	   record until the first of: `ACKNOWLEDGED` — a declared custodian
   585	   (the submitter by default) confirms possession of a record that
   586	   verifies against the commitment value; `DELIVERY_FAILED` — a
   587	   non-retryable delivery failure is established; or
   588	   `DELIVERY_EXPIRED` — the declared bounded retention horizon ends
   589	   without acknowledgment. Failure and expiry are visible terminal
   590	   dispositions, never silent deletion; the retained record's
   591	   subsequent disposition follows the declared minimization and
   592	   retention policy, and that disposition is itself recorded. Emission
   593	   with verified handoff is the discharge moment: after
   594	   acknowledgment, continued availability of the complete record is a
   595	   custodial dependency under the §A3.4 survivability floor, not a
   596	   dependency on the generating authority. The handoff mechanism
   597	   remains open.
   598	
   599	   **Commitment publication.** Publication is a separate state
   600	   machine: `PENDING`; `PUBLISHED`, only on acknowledgment from the
   601	   declared external channel; `PUBLICATION_FAILED`;
   602	   `PUBLICATION_EXPIRED`. Creating the commitment value is not
   603	   publication, and the handoff acknowledgment does not substitute for
   604	   the channel's — conflating the two acknowledgments would hide
   605	   exactly the crash interval this decomposition exposes. The
   606	   publication channel must declare its survival, availability,
   607	   privacy, and inclusion assumptions, **and** the independence of its
   608	   failure modes from the declared refusal triggers — or the
   609	   correlation: A2.3's dominant triggers (fee spikes, congestion,
   610	   calendar outage) are correlated across attempts, and a channel
   611	   sharing those failure modes is most likely to be unavailable
   612	   exactly when refusals cluster.
   613	
   614	   **Observers.** While the authority lives, the submitter and a
   615	   declared operational monitor can observe pending and failed states.
   616	   Observer roles are registered here; the reporting surface is not
   617	   frozen, and refusal reporting is issuance-time operational
   618	   machinery, not §A3.5 tending — the two may share implementation
   619	   later without sharing registration. After `PUBLISHED`, the public
   620	   can observe the external trace under the channel's declared
   621	   assumptions.
   622	
   623	   **The irreducible residual, named.** If the authority dies after
   624	   the local refusal transition but before record handoff and before
   625	   publication, no surviving observer holds evidence of the refusal
   626	   disposition — even the failure statuses die with the authority. No
   627	   construction can make an event known only to a destroyed party
   628	   knowable without an independent participant already inside the
   629	   refusal transition. Tessera minimizes the interval, retries while
   630	   alive, refuses to report the reporting workflow as complete, and
   631	   names this residual rather than disguising it as
   632	   `PUBLICATION_FAILED`. Its consequence bound: the submitter may
   633	   retain evidence of its own submission, but Tessera guarantees no
   634	   surviving refusal evidence in this interval; regardless, under
   635	   §A3.7.1 no artifact lacking terminal-disposition evidence can
   636	   claim protocol standing — the interval costs the auditability of
   637	   the refusal, never the integrity of what can be claimed on it.
   638	
   639	   **Disclosure architecture (unchanged by this decomposition).** The
   640	   base protocol does not require public disclosure of the complete
   641	   record. A deployment profile may require it for a declared issuance
   642	   class. Tessera does not maintain a foundational auditor-membership
   643	   registry: the submitter may disclose its portable record to an
   644	   auditor, who verifies it against the published commitment.
   645	   Service-side copies and identifying metadata follow the explicit
   646	   minimization and retention policy above. This architecture narrows
   647	   Tessera's possession and disclosure role; the amendment makes no
   648	   legal-process guarantee. The commitment construction must resist
   649	   practical guessing or correlation of identifying fields under its
   650	   declared threat model. It corroborates a disclosed record and does
   651	   not reconstruct a lost one; absent a separately registered
   652	   transparency mechanism, it does not prove that every refusal was
   653	   published.
   654	3. **The bridge finding (tracker; non-author review of the
   655	   disposition performed 2026-08-08 — see
   656	   `docs/reviews/2026-08-08-A3-panel-disposition.md`).**
   657	   A2.1's "The rule" sentence states conjunct 3 only; under decoupled
   658	   clocks conjunct 3 does not imply conjunct 2, so the issuer
   659	   evaluates the **full three-conjunct temporal predicate of A2.2
   660	   (the P5 temporal test inside `VALID_STRICT`)** before shipping —
   661	   as the bridge model's Ship action already does. Evaluation of
   662	   conjunct 3 alone is insufficient and is forbidden. Registered here
   663	   as the authoritative prose repair.
   664	
   665	   `[PANEL-DRIVEN REPAIR — 2026-08-08, from the Kimi addendum,
   666	   clerk-verified against the model]` An earlier form of this repair
   667	   said the issuer evaluates "the full `VALID_STRICT` predicate" at
   668	   ship. That overstates the model and the obligation: the bridge
   669	   model's Ship guard is the three-conjunct temporal predicate plus
   670	   the burial-depth requirement (its header registers exactly this),
   671	   and the issuer does not re-run envelope verification (P1–P3) at
   672	   ship. The repair sentence itself is now scoped to match the model
   673	   — the same prose/model discipline this item exists to enforce.
   674	
   675	---
   676	
   677	## A3.8 The assessment must carry its own boundaries (Sol finding 2)
   678	
   679	`VALID_STRICT` attests envelope soundness, never payload truth — and
   680	nothing previously registered prevented a future API or UI from
   681	relaying envelope validity as content validity: the checkmark relay,
   682	the aTLS genus at the semantic layer. Nor may the system collapse
   683	envelope verification, protocol standing, payload interpretability, or
   684	custodial health into one judgment.
   685	
   686	**The base assessment is an unsigned, reproducible computation.** It
   687	reports what the verifier can establish at evaluation time and leaves
   688	the relying party to decide whether those facts meet its requirements.
   689	It carries at least:
   690	
   691	- identities of the input receipt, bundle members, and evidence actually
   692	  evaluated;
   693	- the P4 envelope-verification result;
   694	- protocol standing (§A3.7.1);
   695	- a machine-readable scope for every claim;
   696	- governing specification, policy, trust configuration, and declared
   697	  evaluation context;
   698	- material reasons, observations, unavailable evidence, and recorded
   699	  waivers; and
   700	- payload Representation Information status when requested.
   701	
   702	Custodial health is separately obtainable current information, not an
   703	input to the stateless base receipt verdict. No unqualified aggregate
   704	checkmark is part of the conforming result.
   705	
   706	**Rendering red-bar.** Every Tessera-controlled or conforming
   707	third-party surface that renders a result also renders its scope and the
   708	material independent dimensions. A surface showing a checkmark without
   709	those boundaries fails conformance. Tessera does not claim an adversarial
   710	relay cannot discard the result and invent or strip presentation.
   711	
   712	**Optional signed verification report.** A party that needs portable
   713	attribution, evaluation-time evidence, or tamper-evident transport may
   714	separately attest the complete base result as a distinct typed wrapper.
   715	Its bounded claim is that an identified evaluator assessed an identified
   716	bundle under an identified environment and obtained the enclosed result.
   717	It neither changes that result nor elevates envelope validity into
   718	payload truth. Signer authority, key lifecycle, and report policy belong
   719	to that separate attestation profile; they are not smuggled into the
   720	base verifier contract.
   721	
   722	`[AUTHOR DECISION — ratified -- 2026-08-08]` **Why unsigned, on the
   723	record.** The drafted Sol-finding-2 remedy placed the scope
   724	declaration inside signed verdict bytes. This section replaces it
   725	deliberately: a mandatory signature would introduce a
   726	verifier-signing authority, key lifecycle, and additional trust claim
   727	into the P9 path — and, depending on the selected signer, could also
   728	reintroduce a service dependency — while strengthening nothing about
   729	the reproduced computation itself. The base assessment therefore
   730	remains unsigned. The trade is named: an unsigned result's scope can
   731	be stripped by a non-conforming relay — the rendering red-bar makes
   732	that a conformance failure rather than a cryptographic impossibility,
   733	and parties needing attribution or tamper-evident transport use the
   734	attested report above.
   735	
   736	The relying-party story (§A3.1.4) is the human-facing statement of the
   737	same boundaries; the structured fields are their machine-facing twins.
   738	
   739	---
