    23	## What this amendment establishes
    24	
    25	**The claim.** V1 is a dual-signed timestamp notary for Tessera's own domain, `wamason.com`. A V1 receipt lets anyone,
    26	with no network and no help from Tessera, check four things: that particular bytes were presented to the Tessera V1
    27	service; that both of the service's keys signed them; that two public channels (DNSSEC, and a signed, timestamped
    28	git commit) said those keys belonged to that domain and were allowed to issue during a stated window; and that the
    29	bytes *and both signatures* existed no later than a time T fixed by Bitcoin, inside that window.
    30	
    31	**The adversary.** Anyone who tampers with, forges, strips, swaps, replays, reattributes or backdates receipts;
    32	anyone who captures authority-channel credentials while the signing keys stay secret (A8.3's sense of capture);
    33	and anyone who steals a key or breaks a signature algorithm *after* a receipt was anchored. Against these, V1's
    34	verdicts hold under the registered cryptographic, key-use and chain assumptions.
    35	
    36	**The boundaries.** V1 does not say who submitted the bytes or what they mean. It does not evaluate revocation, or
    37	whether an algorithm or key was still safe when it signed. It offers no standing, renewal, wrappers or degraded
    38	verification, and no lower time bound or priority. It does not hold against an adversary who holds every required
    39	signing key inside that key's window; against whole-author-sphere capture (R-2.15); or against breaks of SHA-256,
    40	OpenTimestamps, Bitcoin history deeper than k, or the SHA-1 links in git. Both keys are under one controller.
    41	
