   183	## A9.3 Obligations carried forward — OWNER DECISION (Q1)
   184	
   185	Each stays registered with its original text. Its V1-era trigger becomes "before the named capability is offered",
   186	and only a later profile offers it.
   187	
   188	| Obligation | Future capability |
   189	|---|---|
   190	| Submitter attestation, per-organisation issuer | **Attributed receipts** (a non-personal D is a precondition) |
   191	| SC row, A8.4 query, W1 in S-STANDING, O-74, R-6.44, TLR | **Protocol standing** |
   192	| P6 status capture, freshness bound F, DNSSEC status capture, revocation-timing vectors | **Long-term validation (T4)** |
   193	| Forward-link successor slot and its vector | **Service succession** |
   194	| E6b, refusal decomposition and both companions | **Portable refusal records** |
   195	| Lifecycle model, P7 wrapper half, A6.1 vector, O-30, custodial parameter schema, RFC 4998 renewal | **Wrapping and renewal** |
   196	| Case (d) eleven, A8.3 slices, A1 §A1.2.1 waiver lattice, A5.3 record, M6, M7 | **Degraded verification** |
   197	| n-ary channels (O-69) | **Jurisdictional diversity (T3)** |
   198	| PQ on the portable leg (A3 §A3.6) | **Production version** |
   199	
   200	**V2 drivers, registered as named future obligations, not commitments.** None is promised, scheduled or gating.
   201	1. **Separation of control:** independently evidenced custodians, each holding one share of a threshold (m-of-n)
   202	   signing set, so that no single controller can mint. It is what would retire C3's single-controller non-claim.
   203	2. **Party identity use cases:** receipts whose evidence concerns the identity of the parties in `content`, beyond
   204	   what cases i/i′ give (V1 carries such evidence but never checks it).
   205	3. **Renewal:** as in *Wrapping and renewal* above.
   206	4. **Revocation evaluation:** as in *Long-term validation* above, and the B(ii) add-on service.
   207	
   208	## A9.4 Revocation and historical security: not evaluated — AUTHOR DECISION B; OWNER DECISION for historical security. A weakening under A1 §A1.1.
   209	
   210	- **Weakened.** For V1 bundles, `VALID_STRICT` evaluates neither P6 condition: `key_authorized(declared_issue_time)`
   211	  against effective revocation, and no revocation effective at or before `max(anchor_time, declared_issue_time)`
   212	  (A4.3). Every report carries `revocation: NOT_EVALUATED` next to the verdict. *Why:* under the registered text
   213	  a verifier that cannot evaluate revocation returns `UNVERIFIABLE` for every receipt.
   214	- **Historical security.** Every report carries `historical_security: NOT_EVALUATED`. V1 does not evaluate whether
   215	  a signing algorithm or key was safe when it signed. The documentation names what a later examiner must supply:
   216	  an authenticated safe-before cutoff per algorithm and per key, compared against T with A2.1's chain-time
   217	  uncertainty (CODEX-REVIEW 8). A PQ leg removes the need for a quantum cutoff; never for a theft cutoff.
   218	- **Issuer duty.** The V1 issuer never re-authorizes a key (R-5.24). V1 defines no revocation publication.
   219	- **Contract for later verifiers.** Missing status capture does not by itself invalidate a V1 receipt: a later
   220	  verifier given a V1 bundle returns the same verdict and values in a declared stable field set; later adverse
   221	  evidence feeds a separate, stronger assessment. **This is not non-repudiation.** Evidence: a missing-capture
   222	  companion returning `INVALID` or `UNVERIFIABLE` must go red.
   223	
   224	## A9.5 Strict-only, standalone; the receipt anchor — OWNER DECISIONS 2026-10-12 except where marked
   225	
   226	- **Dispatch.** Out-of-profile input gives `UNVERIFIABLE / OUT_OF_PROFILE` at dispatch; A4.2 precedence holds inside.
   227	- **H1a's suite.** Forge, tamper, backdate, break-chain, strip-one-signature, out-of-profile, the A9.1 mapping.
   228	  Degradation and wrapper instances move to their capabilities.
   229	- **Receipt anchor.** OTS stamps `SHA256(A_sig)`, tag `tessera-v1/receipt-anchor-sig`, which commits to both frames
   230	  and their exact detached signatures in manifest slot order (ENCODING-V1 §5, D-8, D-14). The verifier verifies the
   231	  committed signature bytes only. The frame-only layout is withdrawn, with no fallback.
   232	- **Issue order (D-15).** Fix manifest and metadata; build frames; sign; verify; **freeze** the signature bytes;
   233	  **stamp** `A_sig` (one stamp per receipt, R-3.42); obtain confirmation; ship. A signature made after T cannot fit
   234	  the anchor. A retry that changes any committed byte needs a fresh stamp. Signature identifiers stay independent
   235	  of signature bytes; the anchor digest may commit to them; nothing is normalized after stamping.
   236	- **Git stamp target (D-16).** The git channel's OTS stamp targets the **signed commit's object id**, never a tree,
   237	  blob or unsigned commit. The verifier recomputes the id, verifies `gpgsig`, follows commit→tree→blob, and checks
   238	  the stamped id. SHA-1 linkage remains a stated residual.
   239	- **Root-KSK rule.** The root DNSKEY set is valid at the snapshot's anchored time **if some trusted KSK whose signing
   240	  period contains that time validates it**. A trusted KSK that is published but did not sign is informational,
   241	  never a failure. The trust configuration records signing periods separately from publication and revocation
   242	  dates. **Rollover control:** a snapshot taken while one trusted KSK is published but not signing must pass
   243	  (SPIKE-REPORT, KSK-2024 re-capture; without it every receipt from 2026-10-11 would be falsely rejected).
   244	- **Custody.** V1 offers no renewal. Long-horizon validity depends on the dependency manifest and on the holder
   245	  keeping the exact bytes (a mirror that alters a signature byte breaks its copy, CODEX-REVIEW 5).
