    88	## A4.2 P4: precedence ratified; one qualifier
    89	
    90	**Precedence — RULED (author).** When one required check fails and
    91	another cannot be performed, the verdict is `INVALID`. This is what
    92	`formal/tla/P4_VerifierStates.tla` implements (its comment, lines
    93	49–52, said "for author ratification"); §A1.2.1 orders the two cases
    94	separately and never the combination. Reason: a definite failure is
    95	stronger evidence than an open question, both outcomes are
    96	fail-closed, and the alternative would make the verdict depend on
    97	evaluation order. The author: *"I consider this to be ratified and
    98	consistent with prior discussion."*
    99	
   100	**Qualifier.** P4's sentence "A check that cannot be performed yields
   101	`UNVERIFIABLE` — never any `VALID` state, under any trace" is read,
   102	and is hereby written, as "A **required** check that cannot be
   103	performed…". §A1.2.1 already limits the unperformable-to-
   104	`UNVERIFIABLE` rule to non-waivable checks and permits explicit
   105	policies to waive declared redundancy whether or not the waived check
   106	could be performed; the model's `ExactDegraded` invariant implements
   107	that; the P4 sentence was looser than both. This is a
   108	claim-correspondence repair in the A3 §2 style, not a weakening: the
   109	operative claim is unchanged.
   125	## A4.4 The gate's third clause: what the written proof is
   126	
   127	`phase-0-prereg.md` §8 requires, before Phase 1a, "the informal
   128	written proof (the author's defend-it-cold account) is in the
   129	repository." Under the author's 2026-09-06 ruling on the scope of
   130	"defend it cold" (the project, its core decisions, and what it can
   131	and cannot do — never per-proof tool fluency) and the suite's
   132	amendment note 5, that account **is the collection of each checked
