   326	- **Malleability now means exact-copy integrity, not forgery.** A signature can be changed and still verify:
   327	  - ECDSA's `(r, n − s)`;
   328	  - an edited unhashed subpacket;
   329	  - another packet serialization;
   330	  - a fresh randomized signature over the same frame.
   331	
   332	  Any such change gives bytes that fail the stamped anchor (`RECEIPT_ANCHOR_MISMATCH`, negative controls XR-10
   333	  to XR-14). So none of them lets a new signature inherit an old time bound.
   334	  - What remains is copy integrity. A mirror that alters a signature byte damages its copy, just as altering a
   335	    frame byte would. Preservation must keep the committed bytes exactly.
   336	  - A later re-stamp of an altered copy gets a new, later time bound. It does not restore the original one.
   337	  - V1 does not require signatures to be unique, and it normalizes no signature (low-s or otherwise). Low-s would
   338	    not cover OpenPGP serialization or unhashed-area changes, and any normalization after stamping would break the
   339	    anchor. Serialization choices are made before stamping (§5.4).
