   356	## 5. The receipt-anchor preimage `A_sig` (G-19 and G-59 resolved; K-2; revised 2026-10-12)
   357	
   358	```
   359	A_sig = header("tessera-v1/receipt-anchor-sig") ‖ u8 0x02
   360	        ‖ u32 len(F_kms) ‖ F_kms ‖ u32 len(S_kms) ‖ S_kms
   361	        ‖ u32 len(F_gpg) ‖ F_gpg ‖ u32 len(S_gpg) ‖ S_gpg
   362	anchor digest = SHA256(A_sig), the value OpenTimestamps stamps
   363	```
   364	
   365	- `S_x` is slot x's detached signature over `F_x`, exactly as §5.2 serializes it.
   366	- Slots appear in **manifest slot order**: `kms` is the manifest's `key_fp`, and `gpg` is `sset[0]`.
   367	- The count byte is the number of slots. It must be 2 (`ANCHOR_COUNT`).
   368	
   369	**Decoding, slot by slot.** Each step's boundary is fixed by bytes already read.
   370	1. `u32` frame length. Above 2^24 + 64 gives `LENGTH_CAP`. Then exactly that many bytes, decoded as one complete
   371	   attestation frame (§3.1). That decode yields the frame's `common.alg`.
   372	2. `u32` signature length. 0 gives `SIG_EMPTY`. Above the cap of the rule for `(common.alg, slot)` gives
   373	   `LENGTH_CAP`.
   374	3. Exactly that many bytes, decoded by that rule (§5.2).
   375	4. After the second signature, any byte gives `TRAILING`. A slot running past the end of input gives
   376	   `TRUNCATED`.
   377	
   378	The rule for a slot is chosen from that slot's own frame and its position, both already read.
   379	
   380	*Reason.* One OTS proof must cover a receipt that has two signed frames (R-3.42 with reading (a)). It must commit
   381	to the signatures, not only the frames: a stamp over unsigned frames proves only that the frames existed. It
   382	leaves open signing them later, once signing capability for every required slot has been obtained (CODEX-REVIEW
   383	finding 1; finding 2 for "every slot"). Length prefixes and whole, exactly consumed parts make the preimage
   384	injective without any hash assumption (PROOF Lemma 7). The tag keeps it from being taken for a snapshot's proof.
   385	
   386	**Outside the preimage.**
   387	- The outputs of anchoring: the OTS proof, txid, block headers and confirmation evidence. Including them would
   388	  be circular (finding 4).
   389	- Transport decoration: armor, containers and the bundle format.
   390	
   391	### 5.1 Why the tag changes (and the count byte does not)
   392	
   393	The 2026-10-09 frame-only layout used the tag `tessera-v1/receipt-anchor`. That tag is **withdrawn and removed
   394	from T**. A frame-only preimage is therefore rejected at its header with `TAG_UNKNOWN` (XA-09). A fully signed
   395	receipt presented with a frame-only anchor is rejected the same way (XR-09).
   396	
   397	There is **no fallback**. No verifier accepts a frame-only anchor under any profile label.
   398	
   399	Reasons:
   400	- **Separation by construction.** With a distinct tag, rejecting the old layout is PROOF Lemma 4: the header
   401	  alone decides it. It does not depend on whether bytes in the old layout happen to fail a signature rule.
   402	  - Under the old tag, that dependence would be real. Under rule R, any byte string of the right length is a
   403	    syntactically valid signature.
   404	  - XA-10 shows the same-tag case: the old layout is rejected today only because the gpg frame's first byte
   405	    fails rule O's packet header.
   406	- **Readable dumps.** A hex dump names which construction it holds (C6).
   407	- **No migration cost.** Nothing was issued under the draft layout, so replacing it costs nothing.
   408	
   409	The count byte keeps its meaning (two slots), so it is not the place to carry the version.
   410	
   411	### 5.2 Signature-slot rules (by algorithm identifier)
   412	
   413	Each slot's `S` is **exactly one detached signature** in the single serialization its rule pins.
   414	- `S` is **nonempty** and **at most the rule's cap**.
   415	- The decoder **consumes `S` exactly**. Bytes beyond the signature's own extent give `TRAILING`, which covers a
   416	  second packet. Fewer bytes give `TRUNCATED`.
   417	- **Never `S`:** ASCII armor, a display string, a keyring, or any container that can hold more than one
   418	  signature.
   419	
   420	The rule is chosen by `(common.alg, slot)`:
   421	
   422	| `common.alg` | slot `kms` | slot `gpg` | Cap per `S` |
   423	|---|---|---|---|
   424	| `OPENPGP4-ECDSA-P256-SHA256` (the classical V1 signers) | rule O | rule O | 1024 bytes |
   425	| a hybrid pair id, registered only if the 21 Oct spike passes (not assigned here) | rule R, ML-DSA-65 (FIPS 204) | rule O (P-256, hardware token; owner decision 2026-10-12 replaced SLH-DSA) | `kms`: exactly 3309; `gpg`: 1024 |
   426	| any other well-formed id (§3.2) | opaque | opaque | 1 to 65536 bytes; no inner rule. Stage (c) gives `UNVERIFIABLE` (canary, A-02) |
   427	
   428	**Rule O (OpenPGP v4 signature, ECDSA P-256, SHA-256; RFC 9580 §4.2, §5.2.3).**
   429	
   430	*The packet header (`SIG_PACKET`).*
   431	- `S` is exactly one Signature packet (tag 2) with a **legacy-format header in its shortest form**:
   432	  - `0x88 ‖ u8 n` when the body has n ≤ 255 bytes;
   433	  - `0x89 ‖ u16 n` when 256 ≤ n ≤ 1021.
   434	- Each of these gives `SIG_PACKET`:
   435	  - any other first byte: an OpenPGP-format `0xC2` header, another packet tag, or ASCII armor's `-`;
   436	  - a longer length form than needed.
   437	- This also excludes partial and indeterminate lengths.
   438	
   439	*The packet body (`SIG_FORMAT`).* The body is, in order:
   440	1. version `04`, type `00`, public-key algorithm `13` (19, ECDSA), hash algorithm `08` (SHA-256);
   441	2. `u16` hashed length, then the hashed subpackets;
   442	3. `u16` unhashed length, then the unhashed subpackets;
   443	4. two bytes: the left 16 bits of the digest;
   444	5. MPI r;
   445	6. MPI s.
   446	
   447	Each of the following holds, or the result is `SIG_FORMAT`:
   448	- each subpacket area is an exact sequence of subpackets, each at least 1 byte, with shortest-form lengths;
   449	- each MPI is 1 to 256 bits, with an exact bit count;
   450	- the body ends exactly after s.
   451	
   452	Type `0x01` (text) is `SIG_FORMAT` (D-10). The codec does not interpret subpacket contents.
